<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Radelchis I of Benevento</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/851">851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Radelchis I was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> from 839 to 851. A former treasurer, he seized power after the murder of Prince Sicard, provoking a long civil war with Sicard's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siconulf">Siconulf</a>, in which both sides hired Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. The war ended in 849 with a formal partition that left Radelchis with Benevento and created the separate Principality of Salerno.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many of the Muslim bands on the peninsula had only tenuous allegiance to the dynasty, serving Naples, the Lombard rulers or <b>Radelchis I of Benevento</b>, and the Emirate of Bari (847–871) had rulers of its own.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A long civil war in the Principality of Benevento ended in 849 with a formal partition: Siconulf became prince of Salerno, ruling the western half including the coast of Campania, Lucania and parts of Calabria, while <b>Radelchis</b> kept Benevento.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-salerno">Principality of Salerno</a></li></ul></section>
